    "cursorSync": "Off",
    "description": "Madrid's surface temperature in July 2026 against its own countryside, from Landsat (30 m, 12:55) and MODIS (1 km, 22:39): by day the city is cooler than the dry fields around it, at night it keeps the heat. Hover a map for a square kilometre's figures; brush how built-up it is.",
    "editable": true,
    "elements": {
      "panel-about": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "",
          "id": 9,
          "links": [],
          "title": "About the data",
          "vizConfig": {
            "group": "text",
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "spec": {
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              },
              "options": {
                "code": {
                  "language": "plaintext",
                  "showLineNumbers": false,
                  "showMiniMap": false
                },
                "content": "**Surface temperature, not air.** What satellites measure in the thermal infrared is how hot the ground, the roofs, the roads and the leaves are, which is not the air you feel. July 2026's clear passes over a 60 km square around Puerta del Sol: four of Landsat 8 and 9 at 12:55, Madrid's clock (30 m; their thermal band is 100 m, resampled by USGS), and the month of MODIS Terra at 11:33 and 22:39 (1 km). Terra crosses earlier every year since its orbit was left to drift in 2020; in July 2026 those were its times over Madrid. Each map and chart is the median of the month, in °C against **the countryside**: the median of the square kilometres under 5 % built-up (ESA WorldCover 2021, 10 m), 49.5 °C at 12:55, 39.3 °C at 11:33 and 24.5 °C at 22:39. **The city** in the cards is the 520 square kilometres at least half built-up.\n\n**Why the city is cooler by day.** In July the countryside around Madrid is mostly dry cropland, grass and scrub (WorldCover), which the midday sun heats more than the city's streets, shaded by buildings and trees, and far more than its parks, woods and rivers. At night the fields lose their heat to the sky within hours, while the city's stone, asphalt and narrow streets give back what they stored all day: the heat island is a night thing.\n\n**The data.** Landsat Collection 2 Level-2 surface temperature (USGS; clouds and their shadows masked by its QA band) and MODIS MOD11A1 v6.1 (NASA LP DAAC; good-quality retrievals only), both read as COGs from [Microsoft Planetary Computer](https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com); [ESA WorldCover](https://esa-worldcover.org) 2021 (CC BY 4.0). Built by `datasets/madrid-heat/build.sh` in [pacha-dashboards](https://github.com/ubica-ecuador/pacha-dashboards): GDAL warps every pass into the frame, numpy takes the medians, DuckDB writes the Parquet. The maps are two one-byte COGs with a colour table, painted by Pacha's TiTiler; the charts load once into your browser (Chaski, DuckDB-WASM) and Mosaic redraws them as you brush.",
                "mode": "markdown"
              }
            },
            "version": "13.2.1"
          }
        }
      },
      "panel-built": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "The more built-up, the cooler by day and the warmer at night: the median of the square kilometres in each tenth of built-up (ESA WorldCover 2021), against the countryside. Drag across it to keep those squares in the profile.",
          "id": 7,
          "links": [],
          "title": "By how built-up the square is",
          "vizConfig": {
            "group": "ubica-chaski-mosaic-panel",
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "spec": {
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              },
              "options": {
                "preaggregate": true,
                "reads": "",
                "spec": "# The median of the square kilometres in each tenth of built-up, by pass,\n# against the countryside. Brush it to keep those squares in the profile.\nparams:\n  cellFilters: { select: crossfilter }\nvconcat:\n  - legend: color\n    for: built\n  - name: built\n    plot:\n      - mark: ruleY\n        data: [0]\n        stroke: currentColor\n        strokeOpacity: 0.35\n      - mark: lineY\n        data: { from: long, filterBy: $cellFilters }\n        x: built\n        y: { median: vs }\n        stroke: series\n        strokeWidth: 2.5\n        curve: monotone-x\n      - mark: dot\n        data: { from: long, filterBy: $cellFilters }\n        x: built\n        y: { median: vs }\n        fill: series\n        r: 2.5\n      - select: intervalX\n        as: $cellFilters\n    colorDomain: ['By day, 12:55 (Landsat, 30 m)', 'By day, 11:33 (MODIS, 1 km)', 'By night, 22:39 (MODIS, 1 km)']\n    colorRange: ['#F2A93B', '#F7D79A', '#7B8CF5']\n    xDomain: [0, 100]\n    yDomain: [-7, 6]\n    xLabel: 'Built-up share of the square kilometre (%) →'\n    yLabel: '↑ °C against the countryside'\n    marginLeft: 36\n",
                "variables": ""
              }
            },
            "version": "2.2.0"
          }
        }
      },
